lyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents the whole plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin. Therefore, it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n- Diagram III is a parabola that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin. Therefore, it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/example-10975.json" "file_sha256" "fa495013c197c2c879161648e67c54e51ae372c3bcce7802e827520cb3adc13a"} {"question" "e-10979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/c66cdc8bbddfb00ce69f843b1ce1d12116b2dd82312a34abafa0fb13907110cf.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/example-10979.json" "file_sha256" "9ea83c7d9d244d9815a8c70ada30be89b9631e38d378f07bec024735ac8980ac"} {"question" "e-10979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I  represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of  $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Diagram II  represents a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is also a subspace of  $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/a80f685053d7ac3f868728be13b5651c15bfb6c8f8f4394df75256b750a67485.png)  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/example-10979.json" "file_sha256" "9ea83c7d9d244d9815a8c70ada30be89b9631e38d378f07bec024735ac8980ac"} {"question" "e-10976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the subsets $\\; A=\\{ \\langle x, y, z \\rangle \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 : x^2=y \\}\\;$ and $\\; B= \\{ \\langle 3-t, -3+t, -6+2t \\rangle : t \\in \\mathbb{R} \\}.\\;$ Which of them is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/example-10976.json" "file_sha256" "b32bcda49f3dbe8da4cc38dfb7376725f44ce24c26c2daf57d354ce4d59d6a6c"} {"question" "e-10976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under scalar multiplication. For example, the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/b0bc18bcfd9d61510bed4ec4126688b599f5d3e6dbc883394ef7d5acbcf935f6.png) belongs to $A,$ but the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/3f87fe8f3e9e256453a5d60981170b1655286081f9987bbeb7efc7624f6c6749.png) does not lie in $A$ (because it does not satisfy $x^2=y$).\n\n- The set $B$ represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/75f1370c0eaafdcab1aa8e4cc673dbe6f71c61a0ad9f69a437ff9d8f4b115c42.png)\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3-t=0 \\\\ -3+t=0 \\\\ -6+2t=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $t=3$ is the solution to the three equations. So, if we choose $t=3,$ then we find that $\\mathbf{0} \\in B.$ Therefore, $B$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/example-10976.json" "file_sha256" "b32bcda49f3dbe8da4cc38dfb7376725f44ce24c26c2daf57d354ce4d59d6a6c"}] "usage" {"e-10979" [] "e-10976" [] "e-10975" [] "q-34318" []} "basis" 2090 "retractions" [[17592186193139 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4077/Source/Images/q-34318.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $R^{2}$?"] [17592186192331 :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^2$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4077/Source/Images/cdd0cbcb7e4fcb26862a5ab33958edac.png)"] [17592186192331 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents the whole plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin. Therefore, it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n- Diagram III is a parabola that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin. Therefore, it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186192333 :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^3$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4077/Source/Images/31239f102a0bab45ca4ad0b3cb2e9ccb.png)"] [17592186192333 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n- Diagram II represents a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is also a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186192332 :question/problem "Consider the subsets $\\; A=\\{\\langle x, y, z \\rangle \\in \\mathbb{R}^3: x^2=y \\}\\;$ and $\\; B= \\{\\langle 3-t, -3+t, -6+2t \\rangle: t \\in \\mathbb{R} \\}.\\;$ Which of them is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$?"] [17592186192332 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under scalar multiplication. For example, the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $A$, but the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ does not lie in $A$ (because it does not satisfy $x^2=y$).\n- The set $B$ represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 - t \\\\ -3 + t \\\\ -6 + 2t\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhich gives us\n${3 - t = 0; - 3 + t = 0; - 6 + 2t = 0$.\nWe see that $t=3$ is the solution to the three equations. So, if we choose $t=3$, then we find that $\\mathbf{0} \\in B$. Therefore, $B$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186191623 17592186191624 17592186191625 17592186191626 17592186191627] "immutable_fields" [[17592186191622 :answer-field/correct] [17592186191622 :answer-field/id] [17592186191622 :answer-field/key] [17592186191622 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
